1
00:00:00,048 --> 00:00:00,417
So

2
00:00:00,995 --> 00:00:04,432
/fork and /clone allow you to continue a

3
00:00:04,432 --> 00:00:07,740
conversation in a fresh copy of a session.

4
00:00:07,884 --> 00:00:10,117
And you can either copy at the end with

5
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:11,144
/clone or

6
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:12,606
earlier with

7
00:00:13,184 --> 00:00:14,372
/fork, but it's a copy.

8
00:00:15,031 --> 00:00:15,255
With

9
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:18,018
/tree, on the other hand, you don't create a

10
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:20,844
copy. Instead, you continue in a new branch

11
00:00:20,908 --> 00:00:23,012
in the existing session. And that can be a

12
00:00:23,012 --> 00:00:26,256
super powerful feature. And the idea behind

13
00:00:26,753 --> 00:00:27,010
/tree

14
00:00:27,556 --> 00:00:30,817
is that sessions are organized as trees by

15
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:31,347
Py.

16
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:33,965
And that means that you

17
00:00:34,704 --> 00:00:36,503
have your conversation, which consists of

18
00:00:36,551 --> 00:00:38,945
messages, tool calls, and so on. And at some

19
00:00:39,121 --> 00:00:42,173
point, you send a message and you follow

20
00:00:42,189 --> 00:00:44,631
down a certain path, but then you can run

21
00:00:45,562 --> 00:00:48,357
/tree to go back to an earlier point and

22
00:00:48,373 --> 00:00:50,911
follow down a new path, but bring a summary

23
00:00:51,007 --> 00:00:52,999
of the changes you made in another branch

24
00:00:53,336 --> 00:00:55,968
along with you to that new point. That is

25
00:00:56,064 --> 00:00:57,123
kind of the idea.

26
00:00:57,829 --> 00:01:00,332
But that sounds a bit abstract until you see

27
00:01:00,332 --> 00:01:03,397
it. So here I'm back in my project and I'm

28
00:01:03,526 --> 00:01:05,868
actually in that forked session where I

29
00:01:05,868 --> 00:01:08,083
forked at an earlier point, but that does

30
00:01:08,115 --> 00:01:10,329
not matter. You can use /tree in any

31
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:12,110
session. So here we have all that

32
00:01:12,110 --> 00:01:14,293
information about that latest feature that

33
00:01:14,293 --> 00:01:16,668
was implemented. And now I wanna continue

34
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,733
working on a new feature. Now I'm at 30% of

35
00:01:19,749 --> 00:01:22,539
my context window. So still plenty of space

36
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:24,575
left, but it's something to keep in mind. It

37
00:01:24,623 --> 00:01:25,681
will become important later.

38
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:27,989
So I wanna continue working on the next

39
00:01:27,989 --> 00:01:29,961
feature and therefore I can ask, what would

40
00:01:30,009 --> 00:01:33,007
be a reasonable next step? If I'm not sure

41
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:34,882
what we should add next, you can ask the

42
00:01:34,962 --> 00:01:36,870
agent because it knows what was already

43
00:01:36,902 --> 00:01:38,793
implemented and therefore it can tell you

44
00:01:38,986 --> 00:01:41,022
what a good next feature would be. And here

45
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:43,602
it suggests that we might wanna add the feed

46
00:01:43,795 --> 00:01:47,145
and basic user discovery follow flow, which

47
00:01:47,145 --> 00:01:49,213
of course makes sense because users can only

48
00:01:49,245 --> 00:01:51,441
have a feed where they see messages if they

49
00:01:51,489 --> 00:01:52,708
can also follow other

50
00:01:53,911 --> 00:01:55,949
So it suggests to implement follows and then

51
00:01:56,061 --> 00:01:58,196
implement the feed, and that makes sense to

52
00:01:58,212 --> 00:02:00,795
me. So I'll ask it to create a detailed plan

53
00:02:00,795 --> 00:02:02,785
for this feature, hoping that this will

54
00:02:02,865 --> 00:02:05,417
invoke our create plan skill. And

55
00:02:06,091 --> 00:02:08,177
that happens, or to be precise, in that

56
00:02:08,177 --> 00:02:10,167
session, of course, the skill was already

57
00:02:10,183 --> 00:02:11,948
invoked earlier, so it's still in the

58
00:02:11,948 --> 00:02:14,243
context. And it therefore goes ahead, does

59
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:17,035
more analysis, does more research, and will

60
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:18,992
potentially ask follow-up questions, and

61
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:21,383
then at some point, create that plan.

62
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:22,184
And

63
00:02:22,922 --> 00:02:23,708
it's done

64
00:02:24,285 --> 00:02:26,915
here. It created an implementation plan,

65
00:02:27,332 --> 00:02:29,722
opened it as HTML for me to review.

66
00:02:30,315 --> 00:02:32,480
And of course we can now therefore implement

67
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:33,089
this plan.

68
00:02:34,244 --> 00:02:34,485
But

69
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:36,762
now we already occupy

70
00:02:37,435 --> 00:02:40,963
almost 34% of our context window. So yeah,

71
00:02:40,996 --> 00:02:43,529
still plenty of space and implementing the

72
00:02:43,529 --> 00:02:46,432
plan here would be fine, but agents and AI

73
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:50,303
models tend to perform a bit worse the more

74
00:02:50,592 --> 00:02:52,986
context you have in a session. I mean,

75
00:02:53,082 --> 00:02:55,267
context is important, but if there's not

76
00:02:55,411 --> 00:02:57,676
that much space left anymore in the context

77
00:02:57,676 --> 00:02:59,845
window, performance can become a problem.

78
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:00,519
Still,

79
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:02,366
we wouldn't have a problem here.

80
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:05,595
But there also is no real reason to

81
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:08,053
implement the plan here. I mean, it's saved

82
00:03:08,053 --> 00:03:10,751
in a Markdown file, so we could easily start

83
00:03:10,751 --> 00:03:11,860
a new session with

84
00:03:12,389 --> 00:03:12,549
/new

85
00:03:13,078 --> 00:03:15,643
or in a new terminal window, we could start

86
00:03:15,659 --> 00:03:16,348
a new session

87
00:03:17,021 --> 00:03:18,737
and we could use our implement plan

88
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:20,821
prompt template, for example,

89
00:03:21,414 --> 00:03:24,395
to point at.plans and then this Markdown

90
00:03:24,395 --> 00:03:26,223
file that was just added,

91
00:03:26,864 --> 00:03:28,660
this one here. We could absolutely do that,

92
00:03:28,724 --> 00:03:31,561
nothing wrong with it. But we could also use

93
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:35,537
/tree instead to stay in this session but

94
00:03:35,537 --> 00:03:39,193
move to a different place in there. To move

95
00:03:39,226 --> 00:03:42,099
to an earlier place potentially where we

96
00:03:42,099 --> 00:03:44,362
have less consumption of the context window,

97
00:03:44,443 --> 00:03:45,438
but still some

98
00:03:46,048 --> 00:03:49,210
context in there that may be helpful for

99
00:03:49,419 --> 00:03:51,233
implementing that plan. So if you run

100
00:03:52,052 --> 00:03:54,700
/tree, what will happen is that this picker

101
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,975
here opens where you can really select every

102
00:03:58,248 --> 00:04:00,993
single turn that happened in your session,

103
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:01,972
every tool call,

104
00:04:02,502 --> 00:04:05,359
every tool call result, every agent message,

105
00:04:05,552 --> 00:04:08,169
every message from you, you can select every

106
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:10,942
place in your session and jump to that

107
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:13,138
place. So you can navigate around in your

108
00:04:13,186 --> 00:04:16,456
session and jump to earlier and later turns

109
00:04:16,536 --> 00:04:16,873
in there.

110
00:04:17,578 --> 00:04:20,623
You can also use these shortcuts to navigate

111
00:04:20,623 --> 00:04:23,012
that tree. For example, the left and right

112
00:04:23,092 --> 00:04:25,480
arrow keys to cycle through these pages to

113
00:04:25,897 --> 00:04:26,971
move a bit quicker through it,

114
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:29,007
or also

115
00:04:29,664 --> 00:04:30,609
Alt or Option

116
00:04:31,507 --> 00:04:34,504
arrow to fold a tree. And right now we only

117
00:04:34,553 --> 00:04:38,017
have one tree, only one path of messages and

118
00:04:38,017 --> 00:04:40,327
of turns in that session. So we can fold

119
00:04:40,327 --> 00:04:40,680
that

120
00:04:41,627 --> 00:04:45,205
and move around. You can also assign labels

121
00:04:46,023 --> 00:04:47,596
with this shortcut, Shift+L.

122
00:04:48,446 --> 00:04:50,147
If you do that, you can give a label like

123
00:04:50,276 --> 00:04:53,308
main, and now this main tree is called main,

124
00:04:53,421 --> 00:04:55,025
if that makes it a bit easier for you to

125
00:04:55,025 --> 00:04:57,272
identify. Because you can also search by

126
00:04:57,272 --> 00:05:00,192
just typing. If I type main, I select this.

127
00:05:00,481 --> 00:05:02,807
That's why assigning names to turns can be

128
00:05:02,807 --> 00:05:05,198
useful. If I type assistant, I can filter

129
00:05:05,198 --> 00:05:07,942
for assistant messages. I can also filter By

130
00:05:07,942 --> 00:05:10,715
using these shortcuts. For example, Ctrl+U

131
00:05:10,972 --> 00:05:13,505
will only show the user messages. Ctrl+A

132
00:05:13,553 --> 00:05:15,428
shows all messages. Ctrl+L

133
00:05:16,102 --> 00:05:18,811
shows the labeled messages. Ctrl+T shows

134
00:05:19,549 --> 00:05:22,370
the main messages without tool calls and so

135
00:05:22,370 --> 00:05:24,470
on. So you can do that. And here, for

136
00:05:24,518 --> 00:05:27,596
example, we could jump back to this

137
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,177
assistant message, which was the first reply

138
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:34,329
to my initial prompt where back then I asked

139
00:05:34,458 --> 00:05:36,622
the agent to analyze the project and make a

140
00:05:36,622 --> 00:05:39,910
plan for adding that posts feature we added

141
00:05:39,910 --> 00:05:40,937
way earlier in the course.

142
00:05:41,595 --> 00:05:44,339
We could jump back there. So before that

143
00:05:44,339 --> 00:05:46,570
feature even was added. Now important, if we

144
00:05:46,570 --> 00:05:49,539
do that, we will not undo the code that was

145
00:05:49,556 --> 00:05:51,851
written by the agent. We're just jumping

146
00:05:51,883 --> 00:05:54,836
back in the conversation. So to that point

147
00:05:54,836 --> 00:05:57,259
in time in the conversation. So of course,

148
00:05:57,324 --> 00:05:59,410
if we do that, the agent will not be aware

149
00:05:59,426 --> 00:06:00,646
of the changes that were made.

150
00:06:01,256 --> 00:06:02,620
But that's where the interesting part

151
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:05,796
happens. If we jump back here, we can ask Pi

152
00:06:05,844 --> 00:06:08,073
to either create no summary, so then we

153
00:06:08,073 --> 00:06:10,654
really just go back and that's it, or we

154
00:06:10,654 --> 00:06:12,723
create a summary automatically or with a

155
00:06:12,723 --> 00:06:15,769
custom prompt. And that allows us to persist

156
00:06:15,769 --> 00:06:18,013
some of the context, some of all the stuff

157
00:06:18,030 --> 00:06:20,547
that happened in between now and the place

158
00:06:20,659 --> 00:06:21,573
we jump back to.

159
00:06:22,182 --> 00:06:24,443
So here I'll use summarize with custom

160
00:06:24,443 --> 00:06:25,485
prompt and I'll say,

161
00:06:26,062 --> 00:06:27,954
summarize all the changes you made, all the

162
00:06:27,954 --> 00:06:30,263
files touched, and that most recent plan you

163
00:06:30,359 --> 00:06:33,020
created, including a path to that plan.md

164
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:33,293
file.

165
00:06:34,144 --> 00:06:36,249
And if I hit enter, that is now happening

166
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:38,531
with help of the AI. Of course, a summary is

167
00:06:38,563 --> 00:06:40,973
being created and we then move back to that

168
00:06:41,038 --> 00:06:43,400
earlier point in time in our session. But

169
00:06:43,930 --> 00:06:46,372
all the other messages, all the other turns

170
00:06:46,437 --> 00:06:48,542
will not be lost. They won't be overwritten.

171
00:06:48,847 --> 00:06:51,707
We just open up a new branch in that session

172
00:06:51,739 --> 00:06:54,166
because that's exactly what tree is about.

173
00:06:54,214 --> 00:06:56,689
You can have multiple branches in a session

174
00:06:57,122 --> 00:06:59,147
and you can then freely move between them.

175
00:06:59,614 --> 00:07:01,998
And you'll see that in just a second. So

176
00:07:01,998 --> 00:07:04,334
here it's done summarizing and you can view

177
00:07:04,463 --> 00:07:06,444
that summary by pressing Ctrl+O.

178
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,956
So here you see the summary it created. You

179
00:07:09,956 --> 00:07:10,458
see that,

180
00:07:10,992 --> 00:07:12,740
for example, it also has that information

181
00:07:12,773 --> 00:07:14,699
that the next step is to implement that

182
00:07:15,265 --> 00:07:16,704
brand new plan it created.

183
00:07:18,512 --> 00:07:20,287
But you also see what happened before, or

184
00:07:20,287 --> 00:07:22,208
that we have that information that it added

185
00:07:22,208 --> 00:07:23,515
all that post-related stuff.

186
00:07:24,177 --> 00:07:26,232
So that's now all there, but very much

187
00:07:26,346 --> 00:07:28,882
condensed. And for that reason, we now only

188
00:07:28,882 --> 00:07:32,074
occupy around 4.5% end of the context

189
00:07:32,074 --> 00:07:34,254
window. So we got way more space available.

190
00:07:34,976 --> 00:07:36,451
Now before we implement that plan, let me

191
00:07:36,483 --> 00:07:38,440
show you tree again though, because now

192
00:07:38,584 --> 00:07:40,284
something changed here. Now we got a new

193
00:07:40,412 --> 00:07:40,813
branch.

194
00:07:41,374 --> 00:07:43,266
We got that branch summary which just was

195
00:07:43,299 --> 00:07:45,896
created, but this opens up a new branch. Now

196
00:07:46,570 --> 00:07:49,296
you see this change here. We got 2 branches

197
00:07:49,296 --> 00:07:51,156
here essentially. That's the old branch we

198
00:07:51,156 --> 00:07:53,882
had with all its conversation. We can cycle

199
00:07:53,882 --> 00:07:56,175
through it and at the end we had that plan.

200
00:07:56,448 --> 00:07:57,843
That's the old branch here.

201
00:07:59,237 --> 00:08:00,968
But if we fold that, we see that we now

202
00:08:01,161 --> 00:08:03,116
opened a new branch beginning with that

203
00:08:03,116 --> 00:08:04,879
summary, which is the summary of that old

204
00:08:04,943 --> 00:08:07,459
branch. But now we can continue in that new

205
00:08:07,587 --> 00:08:09,991
branch based on that summary, based on that

206
00:08:09,991 --> 00:08:12,443
plan that was created in the old branch. And

207
00:08:12,443 --> 00:08:14,238
it's all in one session. And we could jump

208
00:08:14,238 --> 00:08:16,065
back to that old branch if we wanted to,

209
00:08:16,322 --> 00:08:18,453
with or without a summary of any work we did

210
00:08:18,485 --> 00:08:20,777
in between. And we can jump to any message

211
00:08:20,809 --> 00:08:22,860
there too, to the last message, for example.

212
00:08:23,373 --> 00:08:25,409
We can jump there with no summary. Boom,

213
00:08:25,521 --> 00:08:27,075
we're back. We can continue here. And you

214
00:08:27,075 --> 00:08:29,513
see we're back at those 30% consumption.

215
00:08:30,588 --> 00:08:30,877
But

216
00:08:31,535 --> 00:08:34,135
we can also fold this and go to this brand

217
00:08:34,263 --> 00:08:36,654
new summary, also with no new summary

218
00:08:36,687 --> 00:08:38,677
because I already have one, where we only

219
00:08:38,693 --> 00:08:41,726
have those 4.5% consumption and our summary.

220
00:08:42,223 --> 00:08:44,309
And that's the idea. You can use this tree

221
00:08:44,309 --> 00:08:45,080
feature to

222
00:08:45,754 --> 00:08:47,728
evaluate something, to create a plan, to

223
00:08:47,744 --> 00:08:50,119
perform a code analysis, to try something

224
00:08:50,231 --> 00:08:50,777
